"""Book 15 (Research, Data and Risk Platforms) chapter briefs: data for tools/make_briefs.py --book 15.

`uses` lists terms defined elsewhere that the chapter relies on (not redefined); owners in parentheses:
(B7.18) = One Quant Book 7, chapter 18, checked against the multi-line \\index{} harvest of Books 1-13 on
2026-09-28 (2,753 definitions); (B14.5) / (B16.7) = a batch book, by its outline (to be confirmed at the sync);
(ch5) = an earlier chapter of this book.

Ownership. Books 1-13 keep their terms: Book 1 the market-data and post-trade vocabulary (symbology, reference
data, normalisation, sequence number, exchange and receive timestamps, settlement, clearing, novation, DvP, CSD,
position, mark-to-market, realised and unrealised P&L, non-display fee); Book 2 trade reporting, ISDA master
agreement, legal entity identifier; Book 3 API key, rate limit, private key, hot and cold wallets, multi-party
computation, cloud region, event log; Book 4 floating point, bitwise reproducibility, counter-based generator,
algorithmic differentiation and adjoint mode; Book 5 pricing engine, market-data snapshot, reference pricer;
Book 6 risk engine, risk data aggregation, full revaluation, revaluation grid, risk hierarchy, risk limit,
product control, independent price verification, operational risk, segregation of duties; Book 7 point-in-time
data, valid and knowledge time, bitemporal data, as-of join, security master, permanent identifier, identifier
mapping, backtest and fidelity level, simulated clock, fill model, order-book replay, replay parity test,
sim-to-live reconciliation, canary deployment, staged rollout, paper trading, data snapshot, content-addressed
storage, environment lock; Book 9 spoofing, layering, marking the close; Book 10 the exchange simulator and
order-entry vocabulary, order management system; Book 11 kill switch, pre-trade risk check, parameter change
control; Book 12 accelerator, memory-mapped dataset, data parallelism, feature store, training--serving skew,
data versioning, model registry, audit trail, shadow deployment, handoff specification, machine-learning
platform; Book 13 latency, hot path, ring buffer, back-pressure, conflation, input journal, deterministic
replay, sequencer architecture, idempotent processing, drop copy, order gateway, risk gate, property-based
test, fuzz testing, performance regression gate, just-in-time compilation, structure of arrays.
In the batch (by the batch file's rule): Book 14 owns network, hardware, timing, colocation, cloud-networking
and connectivity-contract terms (packet capture, precision time protocol, public cloud, availability zone,
service-level agreement, network segmentation is contested below); Book 16 owns business, organisation,
control-function and limit-framework terms (limit utilisation, four-eyes principle, total cost of ownership are
contested below). This book owns the data-system, storage-format, research-platform, pricing-library
architecture, post-trade-system and software-engineering-practice terms.

No strategy files (the outline gives Book 15 none). Python is the language of the platform (numpy, pandas,
polars, pyarrow, duckdb, the standard library's sqlite3); C++20 where a systems piece earns it (ch. 4's mmap
reader, ch. 9's pybind11 extension) with a Rust twin (ch. 4 reader, ch. 9 C-ABI cdylib via ctypes, no crates).
No servers, no network, no GPU, no cloud account in any test: messaging and databases run in process (ch. 24-25),
GPU and cloud figures come from cited sources (ch. 14, 26). Timings measured on this laptop follow Book 13's rule
(caption names the machine, tests assert only machine-independent properties).

Running project (code/firm/, all new and unique; checked against the 378 existing components on 2026-09-28):
  platmap (ch1)  tickcap (ch2)  colfile (ch3)  tickstore (ch4)  tsbench (ch5)  refdata (ch6)  dataqual (ch7)
  pyscale (ch8)  natext (ch9)  marketdf (ch10)  btengine (ch11)  strathost (ch12)  jobgraph (ch13)
  roofline (ch14)  envaudit (ch15)  paramstore (ch16)  posservice (ch17)  rtrisk (ch18)  payoffdsl (ch19)
  riskgrid (ch20)  tradecap (ch21)  posttrade (ch22)  entitle (ch23)  eventlog (ch24)  tradedb (ch25)
  cloudcost (ch26)  goldtest (ch27)  observe (ch28)  accessctl (ch29)  survpipe (ch30)
The data spine: firm.exchsim's recorded day (Book 10) -> tickcap (raw + normalised) -> tickstore (intraday flat /
Arrow IPC, historical Parquet) -> dataqual -> research (btengine on levels 1-4) and services (posservice from
reports and drop copy -> rtrisk on firm.pricing sensitivities -> riskgrid on firm.riskengine) -> tradecap ->
posttrade; eventlog and tradedb are the plumbing the services use; observe watches the chain.
"""
